  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "The sqing value for a binary string SSS of length NNN is calculated as follows:\n\n\nLet X=0X = 0X=0 be the initial sqing value.\nFor each iii from 111 to NNN, let jjj be the **largest** index such that 1≤j&lt;i1 \\leq j \\lt i1≤j&lt;i and Sj≠SiS_j \\neq S_iSj​=Si​.\n\nIf such an index jjj exists, add (i−j)2(i-j)^2(i−j)2 to XXX, otherwise do nothing.\nFor example, consider S=000110S = \\texttt{000110}S=000110.\n\n\nFor i=1,2,3i = 1, 2, 3i=1,2,3, there is no j&lt;ij \\lt ij&lt;i such that Sj≠SiS_j \\neq S_iSj​=Si​.\nFor i=4i = 4i=4 and i=5i = 5i=5, we find j=3j = 3j=3, and add (4−3)2=1(4-3)^2 = 1(4−3)2=1 and (5−3)2=4(5-3)^2 = 4(5−3)2=4 to the answer, respectively.\nFor i=6i = 6i=6, we find j=5j = 5j=5 and add (6−5)2=1(6-5)^2 = 1(6−5)2=1 to the answer.\nThe sqing value is thus 1+4+1=61+4+1 = 61+4+1=6.\nYou are given NNN. There are 2N2^N2N binary strings of length NNN.\n\nFind the sum of all their sqing values.\n\nThis sum can be large, so compute it modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7.\n\nAs a reminder, a binary string is a string whose characters are all either 000 or 111.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — the length of the binary strings to be considered.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the answer, modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤10001 \\leq T \\leq 10001≤T≤1000",
      "1≤N≤5⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 5\\cdot 10^51≤N≤5⋅105",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 5⋅1055\\cdot 10^55⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
